+- **TSC-1 (OWNER-DECIDED):** the two-site results checker (`src/two_site_results_check_v0_7.py`) stays unchanged and is never used again; its default mode drives The Lott's website and must never be run. No code change. This settles probe finding A5.
+- **MH-1 (OWNER-DECIDED design input for the Mode H, results update and prospective design):** the weekly session per game is: the results update; then a catch-up of every draw that has no forecast, in draw order (predict draw N from draws up to N-1 only, then reveal N and score it); then the scoring of any open prospective forecast; then the forecast for the next draw is frozen and published, so that exactly one forecast is always open. Two labels, kept apart in records and league tables: PROSPECTIVE (frozen before its draw) and CATCH-UP (made after its draw, in order; valid only if the method revision was installed before that draw's date, otherwise ordinary walk-forward development evidence). One wrapper command per game; the owner's updater and the scoring and forecasting code stay separate programs. The league table is cumulative; no method is retuned because of its ranking. The production report cadence (weekly, monthly or on request) is an open choice for that design.
+- **OFF-1 (OWNER-DECIDED; the 31 October 2026 principle reaffirmed):** everything installed runs without GitHub. A prospective forecast's timing evidence is a local, create-only, digest-chained forecast record with timestamps; a GitHub push or an emailed digest is an optional external witness, never required. A catch-up forecast's validity is checked against local registry install records, not Git history. R3-D20's wording about timing proof by GitHub push evidence is to be reworded in that design. Only the results update needs the internet.
+- **EFF-1 (OWNER-DECIDED design requirement):** the weekly path is incremental: append-only forecast and score ledgers, reading only the new runs and the new draw; the league table built from the ledgers; N1 run over the prospective and catch-up draws only. Full walk-forward rebatches use WF-1C's workers. The report's collect stage (it rereads every frozen run record: 22 minutes for Powerball) is a candidate for a cached index in the deferred performance study, with identical results required.
+
+- **FB-1 (OWNER-DECIDED):** if the forecasts for the draws of the week of 9 November 2026 cannot be ready in time, the first forecasts slip a week rather than the work being rushed: Mode H and the design keep their full review and rehearsal, and the catch-up (MH-1) covers any draws missed meanwhile. The draws are weekly: Oz Lotto on Tuesday, Powerball on Thursday.
+- **Terms for that design (recorded, not decided):** "the prospective mode" is the weekly production loop of MH-1; "declaring the system developed" (GOAL-1) means the owner declares the pipeline ready for the released cohort, while later waves continue to add methods. How R3's FINAL mode and its freeze-and-publish rules relate to the prospective mode is OPEN for that design. Because R3 sections 10.1(6), 10.2 and R3-D20 name a GitHub push as the timing proof, a ratified R3 amendment, or a ratified design that supersedes those clauses under OFF-1, must come before the first prospective forecast. Mode H itself is: every released variant forecasts each of the 10 holdout draws walk-forward (each from the draws before it), all frozen before the holdout is opened, then scored once; whether Mode H's ten steps also serve as the first catch-up steps is OPEN. Running the results update for the first time, and opening the holdout (writing `HOLDOUT_OPENED_<GAME>.json`), each need the owner's separate approval at the Mode H stage; who or what writes that record is designed in Mode H.
